                    coach_default:          `Allocation logged.`,
                    coach_r1_morality:      `<strong>That is the right read.</strong> Warmth is already at 72 — you cannot win there. Morality is at 49, down 9 points, and it is the dimension the watchdog is attacking. For a sustainability claim, morality is decisive and the most fragile. Name it as the binding constraint and every euro that follows has a target.`,
                    coach_r1_competence:    `<strong>Competence matters, but it is not the dimension on fire.</strong> At 58 it is under-communicated, not under attack. The credibility crisis is about integrity — morality at 49. Fix competence and the watchdog’s accusation still stands.`,
                    coach_r1_warmth:        `<strong>You are reaching for your strongest muscle.</strong> Warmth is already 72; feeding it more barely moves intent and leaves morality — the actual deficit at 49 — untouched. Which impression is the binding constraint <em>right now</em>?`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Lift everything equally” is how you spend €14M and move nothing.</strong> Žabkar’s point is that sustainability moves <em>specific</em> impressions. Spread the spend evenly and you under-fund the one dimension — morality — that is actually blocking intent.`,
                    coach_r1_loyal:         `<strong>Protecting the loyal base misses where the fire is.</strong> The over-45s already trust Vista. The watchdog post is landing on the under-35s, whose morality read is sliding fastest and whose intent the premium’s future depends on.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>“Buy more reach” is the trap the CFO is bracing for.</strong> Awareness is not the gap; credibility is. More ad weight on a contested claim amplifies the very accusation that is suppressing intent.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>The premium is not the problem — the perception behind it is.</strong> An 18% premium survives when superiority is believed. Cut price and you confirm Vista is not worth it; the fix is morality and competence, not the price tag.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Morality is the binding constraint, the under-35s are the at-risk segment, and you read the chain correctly: a credibility gap is breaking spend → impression → intent. Now every allocation can be aimed at the dimension that actually converts.`,
                    coach_r2_audited:       `<strong>Audited substance is the strongest signal you can buy.</strong> Third-party verification of carbon and sourcing moves <em>morality and competence</em> at once — exactly the two dimensions Vista is short on — and it is the substance that lets you survive a fact-check later.`,
                    coach_r2_community:     `<strong>A community garden feels good and moves warmth — which you do not need.</strong> Vista is already at 72 on warmth. A cause project does little for the morality deficit the watchdog is exploiting. Lovely PR; wrong impression.`,
                    coach_r2_noinitiative:  `<strong>“The existing story is enough” is what got you here.</strong> The watchdog called the current claims vague. With no new substance, any communication you run amplifies a contested message and morality slips further.`,
                    coach_r2_influencer:    `<strong>Borrowing an influencer’s voice for a contested claim is risky.</strong> You hand control of a fragile morality message to a third party — and if the substance is thin, the backlash compounds when it is questioned.`,
                    coach_r2_mapped:        `<strong>That is the investment-to-impression map the research is about.</strong> Audited substance tagged to morality and competence, an earned/credibility channel to carry it, and every euro labelled with the perception it buys. You can now connect spend to intent, not just to reach.`,
                    coach_r2_grabbag:       `<strong>A grab-bag of green initiatives is not a strategy.</strong> If you do not know which impression each one signals, you cannot connect it to purchase intent. Audited carbon data moves competence and morality; a garden moves warmth. Which euro buys which perception?`,
                    coach_r2_warmthtilt:    `<strong>Tilting everything to warmth doubles down on your strength.</strong> It is the most comfortable spend and the least useful — morality, the dimension under attack, stays unaddressed while you polish the one impression already winning.`,
                    coach_r3_grounded:      `<strong>Communication grounded in substance — that is the model.</strong> Because a real share of the budget went to verifiable initiatives, the campaign amplifies a claim you can defend. Morality and competence rise and the comms convert them into intent rather than into a second accusation.`,
                    coach_r3_ungrounded:    `<strong>This is the substance–message gap.</strong> You bought heavy communication on claims you have barely funded. Ungrounded amplification reads as greenwashing — morality actually <em>drops</em>, and you have set up the fact-check in Round 4 to hurt you. Where is the verifiable action behind the message?`,
                    coach_r3_sequenced:     `<strong>Proof before amplification — exactly right.</strong> You waited for the audit and certification to land before turning up the volume, so there is no window where you are amplifying a claim you cannot back. That is how a brand survives a watchdog’s scrutiny.`,
                    coach_r3_loudfirst:     `<strong>Launching loud in week one is the watchdog’s opening.</strong> The certification completes in month four; for three months you are amplifying a claim you cannot yet prove. That is precisely the window the fact-check exploits — and morality pays for it.`,
                    coach_r4_verified:      `<strong>The claim was real, so it held.</strong> You handed over the audit and the journalist’s fact-check confirmed it — morality climbed instead of collapsing. Substance built earlier is exactly what made the signal bulletproof now.`,
                    coach_r4_hollow:        `<strong>The fact-check found nothing behind the claim.</strong> You amplified a message you never funded — morality dropped another notch as the greenwashing accusation is now proven. Communication without substance does more damage than silence.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Spinning a fact-check is the second greenwashing hit.</strong> Reframing the question instead of producing the proof tells consumers there is no proof. Morality falls and the watchdog narrative is confirmed.`,
                    coach_r4_quiet:         `<strong>Going quiet lets the accusation stand.</strong> Silence after a fact-check reads as guilt; morality drifts down while Polje’s transparent story fills the vacuum.`,
                    coach_r4_superiority:   `<strong>Defending the premium on superiority, not price — that is the crux.</strong> You held the line and answered Polje with stronger impressions instead of a discount. The 18% premium survives because consumers still believe Vista is worth it.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>Matching Polje’s 8% cut surrenders the premium.</strong> You have just told consumers Vista is not worth its price — and handed away the margin that funds the brand. The premium is defended by perceived superiority, not by discounting.`,
                    coach_r4_deeper:        `<strong>Cutting deeper than Polje wins a price war and loses the brand.</strong> Vista’s whole logic is a premium built on superiority. Out-discounting a transparent challenger destroys both the margin and the positioning in one move.`,
                    coach_r4_romi:          `<strong>That is a board case, not a campaign story.</strong> Incremental margin tied to spend, morality and intent recovered, the premium intact — the CFO can defend that as a return. Reach was never the deliverable; ROMI is.`,
                    coach_r4_reach:         `<strong>Leading with reach invites the CFO’s rebuttal.</strong> “Awareness is up 12 points and I am unimpressed — you spent millions to move it and cannot tie it to margin.” The board review is about ROMI, not reach.`,
                    coach_r4_spend:         `<strong>“We deployed the full €14M” is not a result.</strong> Spending the budget is an input, not an outcome. The CFO wants the return the spend produced — intent, premium and margin — not the size of the cheque.`,
                    coach_r4_overclaim:     `<strong>High conviction on a claim you never funded is exactly the trap.</strong> Believing harder does not make an unbacked signal true — it just makes the eventual fact-check more damaging. Conviction has to rest on verifiable substance.`,

